1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,080
Here are two clear, colourless solutions, hydrochloric acid

2
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:07,360
and sodium hydroxide, both at twenty one point

3
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:08,320
five degrees.

4
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:11,040
Pour them together in a foam cup, and

5
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:13,760
there's nothing to see, no bubbles and no

6
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:14,640
colour change.

7
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:18,000
But the temperature climbs to twenty eight degrees.

8
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:20,400
So how much heat did the reaction give

9
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:22,480
out, and how could you compare it with

10
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:24,720
an experiment that used different amounts?

11
00:00:25,934 --> 00:00:28,013
First, why does it warm up?

12
00:00:28,574 --> 00:00:32,253
In water, hydrochloric acid is completely split into

13
00:00:32,414 --> 00:00:36,333
hydrogen ions and chloride ions, and sodium hydroxide

14
00:00:36,494 --> 00:00:39,493
into sodium ions and hydroxide ions.

15
00:00:39,774 --> 00:00:42,093
The sodium and chloride ions don't change at

16
00:00:42,174 --> 00:00:45,093
all, so they're called spectator ions.

17
00:00:45,374 --> 00:00:47,933
The only change is a hydrogen ion joining

18
00:00:48,014 --> 00:00:50,093
a hydroxide ion to make water.

19
00:00:50,654 --> 00:00:52,733
The water has less chemical energy than the

20
00:00:52,814 --> 00:00:55,533
two ions had, so the difference is released

21
00:00:55,614 --> 00:00:57,693
as heat, warming the solution.

22
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:02,060
A reaction that releases heat is exothermic.

23
00:01:02,260 --> 00:01:05,540
The chemicals lose energy, so the enthalpy change,

24
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:07,460
delta H, is negative.

25
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:09,940
Twice as much of each solution would make

26
00:01:10,020 --> 00:01:12,580
twice as much water, and twice as much

27
00:01:12,660 --> 00:01:13,260
heat.

28
00:01:13,540 --> 00:01:16,500
So to compare experiments, divide by the water

29
00:01:16,660 --> 00:01:17,340
formed.

30
00:01:17,540 --> 00:01:20,580
The enthalpy of neutralisation is the enthalpy change

31
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:22,580
per mole of water formed.

32
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:26,520
Acids that split completely into ions like this

33
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:29,240
are called strong acids, explained in a later

34
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:29,800
video.

35
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:32,280
With a strong acid and a base that's

36
00:01:32,440 --> 00:01:35,320
also completely split, the reaction is always the

37
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:39,160
same one: hydrogen ion plus hydroxide ion makes

38
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:39,880
water.

39
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:41,960
So the value comes out about the same

40
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
whichever pair you use, about minus fifty seven

41
00:01:45,160 --> 00:01:46,480
kilojoules per mole.

42
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:49,240
Acids that only partly split into ions can

43
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:51,480
give different values, and they get their own

44
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:52,120
video.

45
00:01:53,334 --> 00:01:55,573
To measure it, mix the solutions in a

46
00:01:55,654 --> 00:01:59,093
lidded polystyrene foam cup, called a calorimeter.

47
00:01:59,654 --> 00:02:02,533
Foam insulates, so we assume all the heat

48
00:02:02,614 --> 00:02:04,013
stays in the solution.

49
00:02:04,294 --> 00:02:07,173
Then the heat is q equals m c

50
00:02:07,334 --> 00:02:09,413
delta T, from Year 11.

51
00:02:09,494 --> 00:02:11,653
m is the mass of all the solution,

52
00:02:11,774 --> 00:02:14,453
both together, and one millilitre of a dilute

53
00:02:14,694 --> 00:02:16,773
solution is about one gram.

54
00:02:16,854 --> 00:02:19,893
c, the specific heat capacity, is four point

55
00:02:19,974 --> 00:02:22,533
one eight joules per gram per degree.

56
00:02:22,694 --> 00:02:25,253
Delta T is the final minus the initial

57
00:02:25,334 --> 00:02:26,053
temperature.

58
00:02:27,267 --> 00:02:28,706
Here's the hook experiment.

59
00:02:29,187 --> 00:02:31,826
Fifty millilitres each of one mole per litre

60
00:02:31,907 --> 00:02:35,826
hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide, warming from twenty

61
00:02:35,867 --> 00:02:38,066
one point five to twenty eight point zero

62
00:02:38,147 --> 00:02:39,266
degrees.

63
00:02:39,347 --> 00:02:42,786
Find the enthalpy of neutralisation in three steps:

64
00:02:42,827 --> 00:02:45,746
the heat, the moles of water, then divide.

65
00:02:46,967 --> 00:02:48,646
Step one, the heat.

66
00:02:49,207 --> 00:02:52,006
The mass is both solutions, so one hundred

67
00:02:52,167 --> 00:02:54,846
grams, because the heat warms everything in the

68
00:02:54,887 --> 00:02:55,286
cup.

69
00:02:55,847 --> 00:02:58,486
Delta T is twenty eight point zero minus

70
00:02:58,567 --> 00:03:01,726
twenty one point five, six point five degrees.

71
00:03:02,007 --> 00:03:04,726
So q is one hundred times four point

72
00:03:04,767 --> 00:03:07,286
one eight times six point five, which is

73
00:03:07,367 --> 00:03:10,566
two thousand seven hundred and seventeen joules, or

74
00:03:10,807 --> 00:03:13,126
two point seven one seven kilojoules.

75
00:03:14,334 --> 00:03:16,893
Step two, the moles of water.

76
00:03:17,454 --> 00:03:21,453
Moles equals concentration times volume in litres, and

77
00:03:21,534 --> 00:03:24,733
fifty millilitres is zero point zero five zero

78
00:03:24,854 --> 00:03:25,693
zero litres.

79
00:03:26,334 --> 00:03:29,213
For the acid, one point zero zero times

80
00:03:29,294 --> 00:03:32,413
zero point zero five zero zero gives zero

81
00:03:32,574 --> 00:03:35,373
point zero five zero zero moles of hydrogen

82
00:03:35,454 --> 00:03:38,333
ions, and the sodium hydroxide gives the same

83
00:03:38,414 --> 00:03:40,013
amount of hydroxide ions.

84
00:03:40,734 --> 00:03:43,693
One hydrogen ion and one hydroxide ion make

85
00:03:43,854 --> 00:03:47,133
one water, so zero point zero five zero

86
00:03:47,214 --> 00:03:48,973
zero moles of water form.

87
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:53,080
Step three, divide and add the sign.

88
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:56,440
Two point seven one seven kilojoules over zero

89
00:03:56,600 --> 00:03:59,800
point zero five zero zero moles is fifty

90
00:03:59,880 --> 00:04:01,320
four point three.

91
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:04,200
Delta T has only two significant figures, so

92
00:04:04,360 --> 00:04:05,800
round to fifty four.

93
00:04:05,880 --> 00:04:08,440
The temperature rose, so the chemicals gave heat

94
00:04:08,520 --> 00:04:11,320
away, and delta H is minus fifty four

95
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:12,920
kilojoules per mole.

96
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,080
That's a little less negative than minus fifty

97
00:04:15,160 --> 00:04:18,120
seven, most likely because of heat loss: some

98
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:21,000
heat escaped, so the measured rise was smaller.

99
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:24,728
Twenty five millilitres of one mole per litre

100
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:28,200
sulfuric acid is mixed with seventy five millilitres

101
00:04:28,360 --> 00:04:31,640
of one mole per litre sodium hydroxide, warming

102
00:04:31,720 --> 00:04:34,040
from twenty point zero to twenty six point

103
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:35,080
six degrees.

104
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:39,400
Sulfuric acid gives two hydrogen ions, so zero

105
00:04:39,560 --> 00:04:42,040
point zero two five zero moles of acid

106
00:04:42,360 --> 00:04:45,160
gives zero point zero five zero zero moles

107
00:04:45,200 --> 00:04:46,040
of them.

108
00:04:46,080 --> 00:04:48,680
There's zero point zero seven five zero moles

109
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:50,040
of hydroxide.

110
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:53,000
The hydrogen ions run out first, so zero

111
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:55,640
point zero five zero zero moles of water

112
00:04:55,720 --> 00:04:59,160
form, and the extra hydroxide releases no heat.

113
00:05:00,367 --> 00:05:03,406
But the leftover solution still gets warmed, so

114
00:05:03,447 --> 00:05:05,726
the mass is all of it, one hundred

115
00:05:05,807 --> 00:05:06,646
grams.

116
00:05:06,927 --> 00:05:09,806
Delta T is six point six degrees, so

117
00:05:09,967 --> 00:05:12,446
q is about two thousand seven hundred and

118
00:05:12,527 --> 00:05:13,726
fifty nine joules.

119
00:05:14,127 --> 00:05:17,006
Divide by the moles of water, not acid:

120
00:05:17,047 --> 00:05:19,886
two point seven five nine kilojoules over zero

121
00:05:20,047 --> 00:05:23,326
point zero five zero zero moles gives fifty

122
00:05:23,407 --> 00:05:26,526
five point two, so delta H is minus

123
00:05:26,607 --> 00:05:28,766
fifty five kilojoules per mole.

124
00:05:29,007 --> 00:05:31,486
Dividing by the acid's moles would double it.

125
00:05:32,700 --> 00:05:34,260
So here's what to remember.

126
00:05:34,540 --> 00:05:37,020
Per mole of water, not per mole of

127
00:05:37,180 --> 00:05:40,220
acid: the ion that runs out first sets

128
00:05:40,300 --> 00:05:41,100
the water.

129
00:05:41,420 --> 00:05:43,580
Use the mass of everything in the cup.

130
00:05:43,980 --> 00:05:46,700
And temperature up, delta H negative.

131
00:05:47,100 --> 00:05:49,740
Calculate the heat as a positive number, then

132
00:05:49,820 --> 00:05:52,460
add the minus sign, because the reaction gave

133
00:05:52,540 --> 00:05:53,500
that heat away.

134
00:05:54,700 --> 00:05:55,340
Your turn.

135
00:05:55,900 --> 00:05:58,940
Forty point zero millilitres of one point five

136
00:05:59,100 --> 00:06:02,060
zero mole per litre nitric acid is mixed

137
00:06:02,140 --> 00:06:05,100
with fifty point zero millilitres of one point

138
00:06:05,260 --> 00:06:08,460
zero zero mole per litre potassium hydroxide in

139
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:10,100
a polystyrene cup.

140
00:06:10,460 --> 00:06:12,860
The temperature rises from twenty one point zero

141
00:06:13,340 --> 00:06:15,260
to twenty eight point four degrees.

142
00:06:15,980 --> 00:06:18,460
Use the density and specific heat capacity on

143
00:06:18,540 --> 00:06:22,500
screen, and calculate the molar enthalpy of neutralisation.

144
00:06:22,780 --> 00:06:24,060
Pause here to answer.

145
00:06:34,467 --> 00:06:35,426
Moles first:

146
00:06:35,987 --> 00:06:38,706
zero point zero six zero zero moles of

147
00:06:38,787 --> 00:06:42,146
hydrogen ions and zero point zero five zero

148
00:06:42,227 --> 00:06:46,146
zero of hydroxide, so hydroxide runs out first.

149
00:06:46,467 --> 00:06:49,106
The mass is ninety grams and delta T

150
00:06:49,187 --> 00:06:51,826
is seven point four, so q is about

151
00:06:51,987 --> 00:06:54,826
two thousand seven hundred and eighty four joules.

152
00:06:55,107 --> 00:06:57,666
Divide by the moles of water and round:

153
00:06:57,867 --> 00:07:00,066
minus fifty six kilojoules per mole.

154
00:07:00,627 --> 00:07:03,266
Dividing by the acid's moles uses the reactant

155
00:07:03,347 --> 00:07:04,386
in excess.

156
00:07:05,600 --> 00:07:06,240
Last one.

157
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:09,680
A student repeats the hook experiment, this time

158
00:07:09,760 --> 00:07:12,480
with one hundred millilitres of each solution instead

159
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:15,120
of fifty, at the same concentrations.

160
00:07:15,760 --> 00:07:18,480
Predict how the temperature rise and the calculated

161
00:07:18,560 --> 00:07:21,680
enthalpy of neutralisation would compare with the first

162
00:07:21,760 --> 00:07:22,400
experiment.

163
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:24,080
Justify your answer.

164
00:07:24,720 --> 00:07:26,080
Pause here to answer.

165
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:37,940
Both stay about the same.

166
00:07:38,340 --> 00:07:40,900
Twice as much water forms, so twice the

167
00:07:40,980 --> 00:07:43,460
heat is released, but there's twice the mass

168
00:07:43,540 --> 00:07:45,540
to warm, so the rise is still about

169
00:07:45,620 --> 00:07:46,900
six point five degrees.

170
00:07:47,460 --> 00:07:49,700
And the enthalpy is heat per mole of

171
00:07:49,900 --> 00:07:53,060
water, so doubling both leaves it unchanged.

172
00:07:53,380 --> 00:07:56,580
Saying the rise doubles is the classic slip:

173
00:07:56,660 --> 00:07:58,980
it forgets the extra solution soaking up the

174
00:07:59,060 --> 00:07:59,540
heat.

175
00:08:00,767 --> 00:08:04,526
So for any neutralisation heat question: the smaller

176
00:08:04,567 --> 00:08:07,486
of the hydrogen ion and hydroxide ion moles

177
00:08:07,727 --> 00:08:09,366
sets the moles of water.

178
00:08:09,647 --> 00:08:11,406
Use the mass of everything in the cup

179
00:08:11,487 --> 00:08:14,206
for q, divide by the moles of water,

180
00:08:14,287 --> 00:08:17,006
round, and make delta H negative when the

181
00:08:17,207 --> 00:08:18,366
temperature rises.

